An essay by [[Andrew Blauvelt]], published on Design Observer on November 3, 2008, while he was curator of architecture and design at the [[Walker Art Center]]. It also ran on the Walker's blog: https://walkerart.org/magazine/towards-relational-design

!! The frame

Blauvelt splits the last hundred years of design into three waves:

# ''Form'', from the early twentieth century: the search for a visual language that could be learned and taught rationally.
# ''Content'', from the 1960s: design as the making of meaning.
# ''Context'', from the mid-1990s: design judged by what it does once it is out in the world.

He maps these onto the three branches of semiotics: "from syntax to semantics to pragmatics."

[[Relational Design]] is his name for the third wave. It gives "contingent or conditional solutions" and builds "open-ended rather than closed systems."

!! His examples

Time magazine's 2006 Person of the Year ("You"), Threadless and Wikipedia, the DIY culture of //Make// and //Readymade//, Daniel Eatock's fill-in-the-blank formats, IDEO's methods, Dunne & Raby's social probes, Teddy Cruz's Manufactured Sites in Tijuana, LettError's Twin typeface (it changes with the weather), Lust's algorithmic poster wall, the hackable Roomba, and print-on-demand.

Almost all of them are about people, networks, and software. He says "connected ecologies," but he means networks. He mentions "an ethos of material efficiency," but only through print-on-demand. That gap is the subject of [[Contextual Era]].

!! Around it

* Rick Poynor answered in //Print// with "Strained Relations."
* The form-and-content story before it: [[Deconstruction and Graphic Design]], by [[Ellen Lupton]] and J. Abbott Miller, first printed in a 1994 //Visible Language// issue that Blauvelt edited.
* Read in grad school at [[MICA]], where [[Ellen Lupton]] was teaching these same ideas.
* The Walker's own identity from those years, [[Walker Expanded]], came out of Blauvelt's design studio. See [[Context Is a Place]].

